A judge ruled that the White House had to allow entry to journalists from CNN, MS NOW, and Politico after President Donald Trump had their hard passes pulled. That ruling didn't extend to Air Force One, however, and plenty of people were happy to let CNN know a couple of weekends ago that chief White House correspondent Kaitlan Collins and her camera crew were perfectly free to fly commercial to Tennessee to cover the president's trip there.
CNN's Brian Stelter has what he's calling a scoop on Monday. The White House is refusing to let Politico's correspondent on Air Force One, meaning the propaganda outlet would not be able to fulfill its press corps pool responsibilities.
Scoop: The White House is refusing to allow Politico on Air Force One today, which means the news outlet cannot fulfill its press corps pool responsibilities. https://t.co/NNiK296TLJ
— Brian Stelter (@brianstelter) October 5, 2026
With Politico barred from the trip, the White House offered the publication's seat to others that routinely take turns in the out-of-town travel pool, and many publications declined, sources with knowledge of the matter told CNN. More to come on this...
— Brian Stelter (@brianstelter) October 5, 2026
Is Stelter saying that CNN is one of the other publications that declined to take Politico's seat? You'd think he'd make that clearer instead of going with "sources with knowledge of the matter." In any case, Real America's Voice probably has a TV crew on board and is offering its raw feed to any outlet that wants it.
